WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:11.682
Hi! Which cloth soaks up the most water? Which material is strongest? To find out, we need a fair test. Let's learn how.

2
00:00:11.832 --> 00:00:16.074
In a fair test, we change only the one thing we're comparing.

3
00:00:16.224 --> 00:00:21.450
To compare which cloth soaks up the most water, change only the type of cloth.

4
00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:26.994
The size of the cloth, the amount of water and the waiting time must all be the same.

5
00:00:27.144 --> 00:00:32.778
The less water left over, the more water that cloth soaked up.

6
00:00:32.928 --> 00:00:35.922
A towel needs an absorbent fabric.

7
00:00:36.072 --> 00:00:40.314
A raincoat needs a waterproof material, like plastic.

8
00:00:40.464 --> 00:00:46.986
Pot handles are plastic or wood because heat doesn't pass through them easily, so they don't burn our hands.

9
00:00:47.136 --> 00:00:52.074
Windows use transparent glass so light can come in.

10
00:00:52.224 --> 00:00:58.026
Watch out! If each cloth gets a different amount of water, the test isn't fair!

11
00:00:58.176 --> 00:01:02.538
Then we can't tell if the result is because of the cloth or the water.

12
00:01:02.688 --> 00:01:08.154
Change only the type of cloth and keep everything else the same.

13
00:01:08.304 --> 00:01:18.234
Your turn! To compare which paper makes the strongest bag, what should we change? Pause and think.

14
00:01:18.384 --> 00:01:23.442
Only the type of paper. Did you get it right?

15
00:01:23.592 --> 00:01:32.082
Another one! What must stay the same in that test? Pause and think.

16
00:01:32.232 --> 00:01:40.290
The size of the bags and the things put in, and how we add them, must be the same. Did you get it right?

17
00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:49.242
Here's another! What property should a towel's fabric have? Pause and think.

18
00:01:49.392 --> 00:01:54.714
A towel must be absorbent. Did you get it right?

19
00:01:54.864 --> 00:02:04.218
Try one more! Why are pot handles often made of wood or plastic? Pause and think.

20
00:02:04.368 --> 00:02:11.370
Heat doesn't pass through them easily, so they don't burn our hands. Did you get it right?

21
00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:22.074
Try one more! With the same water poured, what does the cloth with the least water left tell us? Pause and think.

22
00:02:22.224 --> 00:02:29.010
It soaked up the most water, so it's the most absorbent. Did you get it right?

23
00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:40.218
Last one! When we compare which cloth absorbs the most water, how much water goes on each cloth? Pause and think.

24
00:02:40.368 --> 00:02:49.098
The same amount each time. We change only the kind of cloth, so the test is fair. Did you get it right?

25
00:02:49.248 --> 00:02:59.010
Remember: a fair test changes only the thing being compared and keeps everything else the same. We choose materials for the properties an object needs.

26
00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:04.674
At home, compare which paper towel soaks up the most water!

